    "body": "By Charlotte Goldstone\n28 October 2026\nThe global express and parcels market grew 5.8% in 2025, to €577.3bn ($642.6bn), but mounting pricing pressure, shifting trade flows and intensifying competition are creating a growing challenge for carriers seeking to turn higher volumes into profits.\nThe recent Global Express & Parcels 2026 report by Transport Intelligence predicted that the market would reach €609.1bn this year and €760.1bn by 2030, representing compound annual growth of around 5.7%.\nBut the headline growth masks stark regional differences.\nAsia Pacific, which in 2024 overtook North America as the largest regional market, is forecast to expand at an annual rate of 8% between 2026 and 2030, while North America is expected to grow 4.1%, and Europe by 3.6%, with several major carriers in mature markets experiencing revenue declines.\nDHL Express told The Loadstar international cross-border express, particularly time-definite services, remained a growth engine for the group, although its premium proposition was not suited to every B2C shipment.\nThe company reported continued strong volumes into Europe and an uplift in shipments to Latin America, as retailers adjusted their strategies following the removal of the US de minimis exemption for low-value packages. Asia-US B2C volumes, which initially declined after the change, subsequently partially recovered.\nunder its Strategy 2030, DHL Express identified 22 markets with above-average growth potential, including Vietnam, Thailand, Poland, Mexico, Turkey, Brazil, and India, citing opportunities linked to near-shoring, supply chain diversification, and investment.\nTi report author Paul Chapman said: “The global parcels market continues to grow, but this masks increasingly divergent performance among operators, particularly in mature markets where pricing pressure, subdued B2B demand, and the shift towards lower-cost services are weighing on revenues.”\nHe added that established carriers faced the challenge of converting rising parcel volumes into profitable growth amid intensifying competition and the expansion of in-house delivery networks.\nThis has meant consolidation gathering pace. Ti tracked 136 mergers, acquisitions and investment transactions between May 2023 and August 2026, including the FedEx-Advent consortium’s €7.8bn agreement to take InPost private. Meanwhile, 24 of 34 identified artificial intelligence use cases in express and parcels are already operational.\nOn margins, DHL Express told The Loadstar it maintained a strong focus on yield and cost management, arguing that customers continued to pay for speed, reliability, security, and customs expertise.\nA spokesperson said: “One area where we have seen a significant increase is in heavier-weight goods, with customers that previously might have favoured a forwarding-air cargo solution choosing DHL Express, based on factors such as the guaranteed uplift and capacity, reduced time for inventory in transit, reliable end-to-end delivery and visibility, and transparent, predictable pricing.”\nAccording to Ti, the B2C segment accounted for 62.8% of global market value in 2025, forecast to rise to 67.3% by 2030. Global parcel volumes are expected to reach 432.5bn by the end of the decade, with Asia Pacific accounting for 80.3% of global volume in 2025.\nThe report also suggests that although the global market will reach €760.1bn by 2030, the growing divide between volume growth and profitable growth looks set to remain a defining challenge for the sector.",
